 *   **Volume Attenuation:** Calculated using Beer-Lambert absorption [S1].
 
+**Pitfalls:**
+*   **Non-telecentricity:** Radon-based methods assume parallel rays and fail to account for non-telecentric error along the vial axis, limiting vertical build volume [S1]. 3DRT corrects this by modeling actual ray divergence [S1].
+*   **Etendue effects:** High etendue causes astigmatism and radial dose variation, leading to uneven curing (e.g., specific letters forming later) if uncorrected [S1]. 3DRT compensates by casting multiple non-chief rays [S1].
+*   **Computational cost:** Modeling complex optics and multiple rays per pixel increases computation time by 10 to 100 times compared to Radon methods [S1].
